# More legends of Wales (added 2026-11-11). Same shape as LEGENDS in features.py:
# id, era (colour), art (a scene kind in art.py), place, titles, one-line summaries,
# the tale in English and Welsh, and the history behind it (hEn / hCy).
# Arthur is never a legend on this site (EDITORIAL.md): where he appears in an old tale
# he is named with respect, as the tale itself names him, and the tale is about someone else.
# GROUPS sets the sections and the order of the Legends page; features.py follows it.

LEGENDS = [
 dict(id="pwyll", era="ancient", art="horse", place="Arberth · Glyn Cuch, Dyfed",
  en="Pwyll and Rhiannon", cy="Pwyll a Rhiannon",
  sumEn="A prince of Dyfed rules the Otherworld for a year, then chases a lady on a white horse whom no one can catch.",
  sumCy="Mae tywysog Dyfed yn teyrnasu yn Annwn am flwyddyn, yna’n ymlid arglwyddes ar farch gwyn na all neb ei dal.",
  pEn=["Pwyll, prince of Dyfed, was hunting in Glyn Cuch when he saw a pack of strange hounds pull down a stag. They were shining white, with red ears. He drove them off and set his own hounds on the kill. Then a grey rider came out of the wood: Arawn, king of Annwn, the Otherworld. To make amends, Pwyll agreed to take Arawn’s shape and rule his kingdom for a year, and to face Arawn’s enemy Hafgan at the ford.",
   "For a year Pwyll ruled Annwn so well that no one guessed. Each night he lay beside Arawn’s queen, and each night he turned his face to the wall and did not touch her. At the ford he struck Hafgan one blow and would not strike a second, for a second blow would heal him. When the year was out, Arawn found his kingdom made whole and his honour kept. The two became friends for life, and Pwyll was called Pen Annwn, Head of the Otherworld.",
   "## The rider on the white horse",
   "Near his court at Arberth was a mound, Gorsedd Arberth. It was said that whoever sat on it would either take a beating or see a wonder. Pwyll sat, and saw a lady in shining gold on a tall white horse, riding slowly along the road. He sent his swiftest rider after her, but the faster he rode, the further off she was, though her horse never left its gentle pace. On the third day Pwyll rode after her himself, and when he could not close the gap he called out: ‘Lady, for the sake of the man you love best, wait for me.’",
   "‘I will, gladly,’ she said, ‘and it would have been better for your horse if you had asked long ago.’ She was Rhiannon, daughter of Hefeydd Hen, and she had come to find him, because of all men she would have him. After a year, and a great deal of cleverness at her wedding feast, they were married.",
   "Their son vanished on the night he was born, and Rhiannon was wrongly blamed. For years she sat by the horse block at the gate of Arberth and offered to carry every visitor to the court on her back. But in Gwent, a lord named Teyrnon had found a baby boy in his stable on May Eve, the night his mare foaled, and raised him. When he saw how the boy grew to look like Pwyll, he brought him home. ‘My worry is over,’ said Rhiannon, and from her word for worry, pryder, the boy was named Pryderi."],
  pCy=["Roedd Pwyll, tywysog Dyfed, yn hela yng Nglyn Cuch pan welodd haid o gŵn dieithr yn tynnu carw i’r llawr. Roeddent yn wyn disglair, a’u clustiau’n goch. Gyrrodd hwy ymaith a gosod ei gŵn ei hun ar yr ysglyfaeth. Yna daeth marchog llwyd o’r coed: Arawn, brenin Annwn. I wneud iawn, cytunodd Pwyll i gymryd pryd a gwedd Arawn a llywodraethu ei deyrnas am flwyddyn, a chwrdd â gelyn Arawn, Hafgan, wrth y rhyd.",
   "Am flwyddyn rheolodd Pwyll Annwn mor dda fel na amheuodd neb ddim. Bob nos gorweddai wrth ochr brenhines Arawn, a phob nos trôi ei wyneb at y pared heb ei chyffwrdd. Wrth y rhyd trawodd Hafgan un ergyd ac ni fynnai daro’r ail, oherwydd byddai ail ergyd yn ei iacháu. Ar ddiwedd y flwyddyn cafodd Arawn ei deyrnas yn gyfan a’i anrhydedd yn ddianaf. Buont yn gyfeillion am oes, a galwyd Pwyll yn Ben Annwn.",
   "## Y farchoges ar y march gwyn",
   "Ger ei lys yn Arberth roedd twmpath, Gorsedd Arberth. Dywedid y câi pwy bynnag a eisteddai arni naill ai ei guro neu weld rhyfeddod. Eisteddodd Pwyll, a gwelodd wraig mewn eurwisg ddisglair ar farch gwyn tal, yn marchogaeth yn araf ar hyd y ffordd. Anfonodd ei farchog cyflymaf ar ei hôl, ond po gyflymaf y marchogai, pellaf yn y byd oedd hi, er na newidiodd ei march ei gam hamddenol. Ar y trydydd dydd marchogodd Pwyll ar ei hôl ei hun, a phan na allai gau’r bwlch, galwodd: ‘Forwyn, er mwyn y gŵr a geri fwyaf, aros amdanaf.’",
   "‘Arhosaf yn llawen,’ meddai hi, ‘a gwell fuasai i’th farch pe gofynnaset ers meitin.’ Rhiannon oedd hi, merch Hefeydd Hen, ac roedd wedi dod i chwilio amdano, oherwydd ef a fynnai o blith pob gŵr. Ymhen blwyddyn, a chryn dipyn o gyfrwystra yn eu gwledd briodas, priodwyd hwy.",
   "Diflannodd eu mab y noson y’i ganed, a rhoddwyd y bai ar gam ar Riannon. Am flynyddoedd eisteddai wrth yr esgynfaen ger porth Arberth gan gynnig cario pob ymwelydd i’r llys ar ei chefn. Ond yng Ngwent roedd arglwydd o’r enw Teyrnon wedi cael bachgen bach yn ei stabl Nos Galan Mai, y noson y bwriodd ei gaseg ebol, ac wedi’i fagu. Pan welodd mor debyg i Bwyll yr oedd y bachgen yn tyfu, daeth ag ef adref. ‘Dyna ben ar fy mhryder,’ meddai Rhiannon, ac o’r gair hwnnw, pryder, cafodd y bachgen yr enw Pryderi."],
  hEn="This is the First Branch of the Mabinogi, the four linked tales at the heart of early Welsh storytelling. They were written down between about 1060 and 1200 from tales told aloud for centuries, and survive in the White Book of Rhydderch (about 1350) and the Red Book of Hergest (about 1400). Arberth is Narberth in Pembrokeshire, and the Cuch still runs into the Teifi on the old border of Dyfed. Many scholars hear in Rhiannon’s name the old British Rigantona, ‘great queen’, and in her horse an echo of a goddess honoured across the land of the Britons long before the tale was written.",
  hCy="Dyma Gainc Gyntaf y Mabinogi, y pedair chwedl gysylltiedig sydd wrth galon hen chwedloniaeth Cymru. Fe’u rhoddwyd ar femrwn rhwng tua 1060 a 1200 o chwedlau a adroddwyd ar lafar am ganrifoedd, ac maent wedi goroesi yn Llyfr Gwyn Rhydderch (tua 1350) a Llyfr Coch Hergest (tua 1400). Arberth yw tref Arberth yn Sir Benfro, ac mae afon Cuch yn dal i lifo i’r Teifi ar hen ffin Dyfed. Mae llawer o ysgolheigion yn clywed yn enw Rhiannon yr hen enw Brythonig Rigantona, ‘brenhines fawr’, ac yn ei march adlais o dduwies a anrhydeddwyd ar draws gwlad y Brythoniaid ymhell cyn ysgrifennu’r chwedl."),

 dict(id="manawydan", era="ancient", art="mist", place="Arberth, Dyfed",
  en="Manawydan and the enchantment of Dyfed", cy="Manawydan a hud ar Ddyfed",
  sumEn="A mist falls, the whole of Dyfed is emptied, and a patient prince wins it back with nothing but his wits.",
  sumCy="Daw niwl, mae Dyfed gyfan yn wag, ac mae tywysog amyneddgar yn ei hennill yn ôl â dim ond ei synnwyr.",
  pEn=["After the war in Ireland, Manawydan son of Llŷr, brother of Brân the Blessed, came home with his friend Pryderi. Pryderi gave him the seven cantrefs of Dyfed and his mother Rhiannon for a wife, and the four of them, with Pryderi’s wife Cigfa, lived happily at Arberth.",
   "One evening they sat on Gorsedd Arberth. There was a peal of thunder and a mist so thick that no one could see the others. When it lifted, Dyfed was empty. There were no people, no cattle, no smoke from any house: only the four of them, and the wild beasts. They went to England and earned their bread as saddlers, then shield-makers, then shoemakers, and they were so good at it that the local craftsmen plotted to kill them. Pryderi wanted to fight. Manawydan would not. ‘We would get a bad name,’ he said, and they moved on.",
   "Back in Dyfed, Pryderi’s hounds chased a shining white boar into a fort that had never stood there before. Against Manawydan’s advice Pryderi went in. He found a golden bowl hanging on chains that rose up out of sight, and when he touched it his hands stuck fast, and his feet, and he could not speak. Rhiannon went to find him and was caught the same way. With a clap of thunder the fort vanished, and they with it.",
   "## The mouse on the gallows",
   "Manawydan and Cigfa stayed. He sowed three fields of wheat, and as each one ripened it was stripped in a night. He kept watch and saw an army of mice. He caught the slowest, and set up a little gallows on Gorsedd Arberth to hang it. A poor scholar rode by and offered a pound for its life. A priest offered three. A bishop with all his train offered twenty-four, then horses, then anything he liked. Manawydan held firm until the bishop told the truth: he was Llwyd son of Cil Coed, the mouse was his wife, and the spell on Dyfed was his revenge for an old wrong done to a friend of his at Rhiannon’s wedding.",
   "Then Manawydan named his price: Rhiannon and Pryderi set free, the spell lifted from the seven cantrefs, and a promise that no revenge would ever again be taken on any of them. Llwyd agreed. Manawydan looked up, and saw the houses and the herds and the people of Dyfed, all as they had been."],
  pCy=["Wedi’r rhyfel yn Iwerddon, daeth Manawydan fab Llŷr, brawd Bendigeidfran, adref gyda’i gyfaill Pryderi. Rhoddodd Pryderi iddo saith cantref Dyfed a’i fam, Rhiannon, yn wraig, a bu’r pedwar ohonynt, gyda Chigfa gwraig Pryderi, yn byw’n ddedwydd yn Arberth.",
   "Un noson eisteddent ar Orsedd Arberth. Daeth taran a niwl mor drwchus fel na welai neb y lleill. Pan gododd, roedd Dyfed yn wag. Nid oedd na phobl na gwartheg na mwg o’r un tŷ: dim ond y pedwar ohonynt, a’r anifeiliaid gwyllt. Aethant i Loegr ac ennill eu bara fel cyfrwywyr, yna fel gwneuthurwyr tarianau, yna fel cryddion, ac roeddent mor fedrus nes i grefftwyr y lle gynllwynio i’w lladd. Roedd Pryderi am ymladd. Ni fynnai Manawydan. ‘Caem enw drwg,’ meddai, ac aethant ymlaen.",
   "Yn ôl yn Nyfed, ymlidiodd cŵn Pryderi faedd gwyn disglair i mewn i gaer na fu yno erioed o’r blaen. Yn groes i gyngor Manawydan aeth Pryderi i mewn. Cafodd yno ffiol aur yn hongian ar gadwyni a godai o’r golwg, a phan gyffyrddodd hi glynodd ei ddwylo wrthi, a’i draed, ac ni allai siarad. Aeth Rhiannon i chwilio amdano a chafodd ei dal yn yr un modd. Gyda tharan diflannodd y gaer, a hwythau gyda hi.",
   "## Y llygoden ar y crocbren",
   "Arhosodd Manawydan a Chigfa. Heuodd dri chae o wenith, ac wrth i bob un aeddfedu fe’i diosgwyd mewn noson. Gwyliodd, a gwelodd fyddin o lygod. Daliodd yr arafaf, a chodi crocbren fach ar Orsedd Arberth i’w chrogi. Marchogodd ysgolhaig tlawd heibio a chynnig punt am ei bywyd. Cynigiodd offeiriad dair. Cynigiodd esgob â’i holl osgordd bedair punt ar hugain, yna meirch, yna beth bynnag a fynnai. Daliodd Manawydan ei dir nes i’r esgob ddweud y gwir: Llwyd fab Cil Coed ydoedd, ei wraig oedd y llygoden, a dial am hen gam a wnaed i’w gyfaill yng ngwledd briodas Rhiannon oedd yr hud ar Ddyfed.",
   "Yna enwodd Manawydan ei bris: rhyddhau Rhiannon a Phryderi, codi’r hud oddi ar y saith cantref, ac addewid na ddielid ar yr un ohonynt byth eto. Cytunodd Llwyd. Edrychodd Manawydan i fyny, a gwelodd dai a gyrroedd a phobl Dyfed, i gyd fel y buont."],
  hEn="This is the Third Branch of the Mabinogi. Its hero wins without striking a blow: he is patient, skilled with his hands, and never loses his head, which is why many readers think it the wisest of the four tales. Manawydan’s name is linked with Manaw, the old name for the Isle of Man, and with the Irish sea-lord Manannán mac Lir, a sign of how closely the peoples around the Irish Sea shared their stories. Arberth, where Gorsedd Arberth stood, is Narberth in Pembrokeshire.",
  hCy="Dyma Drydedd Gainc y Mabinogi. Mae ei harwr yn ennill heb daro’r un ergyd: mae’n amyneddgar, yn grefftwr medrus, ac nid yw byth yn colli ei ben, a dyna pam y cred llawer mai hon yw’r ddoethaf o’r pedair cainc. Cysylltir enw Manawydan â Manaw, hen enw Ynys Manaw, ac ag arglwydd y môr yn Iwerddon, Manannán mac Lir, arwydd o mor agos y rhannai pobloedd Môr Iwerddon eu straeon. Arberth, lle safai Gorsedd Arberth, yw tref Arberth yn Sir Benfro."),

 dict(id="culhwch", era="saints", art="boar", place="Preseli · Porth Clais · Afon Hafren",
  en="Culhwch and Olwen, and the hunt for Twrch Trwyth", cy="Culhwch ac Olwen, a hela’r Twrch Trwyth",
  sumEn="To win the giant’s daughter, a young prince must do forty impossible things, the last of them a hunt across the whole of south Wales.",
  sumCy="I ennill merch y cawr rhaid i dywysog ifanc gyflawni deugain o bethau amhosibl, a’r olaf yn helfa ar draws de Cymru gyfan.",
  pEn=["Culhwch was born in a pig run, and that is how he got his name. When he was grown, his stepmother laid a fate on him: he would never marry unless he won Olwen, daughter of Ysbaddaden Chief Giant. Culhwch had never seen her, but at the sound of her name his face flushed and love for her filled every part of him.",
   "He rode to the court of his cousin Arthur, chief of the princes of this island, on a grey steed with a gold-hilted sword at his side and two silver spears in his hand, with two greyhounds leaping about him. He asked for help, naming every great warrior of the court as his guarantors, and the best of them rode with him: Cai, Bedwyr, Gwalchmai, Gwrhyr Interpreter of Tongues, who spoke every language of men and beasts, and Menw, who could make them all invisible.",
   "They found Olwen. Four white clovers sprang up wherever she stepped, and that is why she was called Olwen, ‘white track’. Her father the giant could not lift his own eyelids, and his servants had to prop them open with forks. To every request he answered with a task, forty of them, each harder than the last.",
   "## The great boar",
   "The hardest was this: the giant’s hair could only be dressed with the comb and shears that lay between the ears of Twrch Trwyth, a king who had been turned into a monstrous boar. The hunt began in Ireland. The boar swam to Porth Clais by St Davids and tore across Dyfed, over the Preseli hills and through Cwm Cerwyn, where it turned and killed some of the best of the hunters. It ran on across the Amman and Loughor valleys and the Black Mountain to the mouth of the Severn, where the shears and the razor were taken, and into Cornwall, where the comb was won at last. Then the boar went into the sea, and no one knows where it went.",
   "With all the tasks done, the giant’s power was broken. That night Culhwch slept with Olwen, and she was his only wife for as long as he lived."],
  pCy=["Mewn twlc moch y ganed Culhwch, a dyna sut y cafodd ei enw. Pan dyfodd yn ddyn, tynghedodd ei lysfam ef: ni phriodai byth oni enillai Olwen, merch Ysbaddaden Bencawr. Nid oedd Culhwch erioed wedi’i gweld, ond wrth glywed ei henw gwridodd, a llanwodd cariad ati bob aelod ohono.",
   "Marchogodd i lys ei gefnder Arthur, penteyrnedd yr ynys hon, ar orwydd llwyd a chleddyf eurddwrn ar ei glun a dwy waywffon arian yn ei law, a dau filgi’n llamu o’i gwmpas. Gofynnodd am gymorth, gan enwi pob rhyfelwr mawr yn y llys yn feichiau, ac aeth y goreuon ohonynt gydag ef: Cai, Bedwyr, Gwalchmai, Gwrhyr Gwalstawd Ieithoedd, a siaradai bob iaith dynion ac anifeiliaid, a Menw, a allai eu gwneud oll yn anweledig.",
   "Cawsant hyd i Olwen. Codai pedair meillionen wen lle bynnag y camai, a dyna pam y’i galwyd yn Olwen. Ni allai ei thad y cawr godi ei amrannau ei hun, a rhaid oedd i’w weision eu dal yn agored â ffyrch. I bob cais atebai â thasg, deugain ohonynt, pob un yn galetach na’r llall.",
   "## Y baedd mawr",
   "Y galetaf oedd hon: ni ellid trin gwallt y cawr ond â’r crib a’r gwellau a orweddai rhwng dwy glust Twrch Trwyth, brenin a drowyd yn faedd anferth. Dechreuodd yr helfa yn Iwerddon. Nofiodd y baedd i Borth Clais ger Tyddewi a rhuthro ar draws Dyfed, dros y Preseli a thrwy Gwm Cerwyn, lle trodd a lladd rhai o oreuon yr helwyr. Rhedodd ymlaen ar draws Dyffryn Aman a Dyffryn Llwchwr a’r Mynydd Du at aber Hafren, lle cipiwyd y gwellau a’r ellyn, ac i Gernyw, lle enillwyd y crib o’r diwedd. Yna aeth y baedd i’r môr, ac ni ŵyr neb i ble yr aeth.",
   "Wedi cyflawni’r holl dasgau, torrwyd grym y cawr. Y noson honno cysgodd Culhwch gydag Olwen, a hi fu ei unig wraig tra bu fyw."],
  hEn="Culhwch ac Olwen, written down around 1100, is the oldest tale in any language in which Arthur’s court appears, and it is wholly Welsh: it places Arthur’s court at Celli Wig in Cornwall and fills it with more than two hundred names from older Welsh poetry. The route of the boar is a real journey you can follow on a map of south Wales, from Porth Clais to the Severn. The hunt is older still: the Historia Brittonum of about 829 tells of the boar Troynt and of a stone in Buellt marked with the footprint of Cafall, the great hound, at the place still called Carn Gafallt. Arthur himself has his own page on this site, ‘Arthur, King of the Britons’.",
  hCy="Culhwch ac Olwen, a ysgrifennwyd tua 1100, yw’r chwedl hynaf mewn unrhyw iaith lle mae llys Arthur yn ymddangos, ac mae’n gwbl Gymreig: mae’n gosod llys Arthur yng Nghelli Wig yng Nghernyw ac yn ei lenwi â dros ddau gant o enwau o hen farddoniaeth Gymraeg. Mae llwybr y baedd yn daith go iawn y gallwch ei dilyn ar fap o dde Cymru, o Borth Clais i Hafren. Mae’r helfa’n hŷn eto: mae’r Historia Brittonum, tua 829, yn sôn am y baedd Troynt ac am faen ym Muellt ag ôl troed Cafall, y ci mawr, arno, yn y lle a elwir o hyd yn Garn Gafallt. Mae gan Arthur ei hun ei dudalen ei hun ar y wefan hon, ‘Arthur, Brenin y Brythoniaid’."),

 dict(id="macsen", era="roman", art="roman", place="Caernarfon · Segontium",
  en="The Dream of Macsen Wledig", cy="Breuddwyd Macsen Wledig",
  sumEn="An emperor of Rome dreams of a maiden in a fort at the far end of the world, and finds her in Arfon.",
  sumCy="Mae ymerawdwr Rhufain yn breuddwydio am forwyn mewn caer ym mhen draw’r byd, ac yn ei chael yn Arfon.",
  pEn=["Macsen Wledig was emperor of Rome. One hot day, out hunting, he lay down by a river to sleep, and he dreamed. He travelled up the river to the highest mountain in the world, crossed it, and came down through a great land to the mouth of a river where a fleet lay waiting. He sailed to the fairest of islands, crossed it from sea to sea, and came to a fort at a river mouth facing another island across the water.",
   "In the fort was a hall with a roof of gold. Two youths sat playing gwyddbwyll on a silver board with golden pieces. A grey-haired man sat on a throne of ivory, carving new pieces. And on a chair of red gold sat a maiden so beautiful that it was no easier to look at her than at the sun. Macsen took her in his arms, and then the hounds bayed and the spears rattled, and he woke.",
   "He could not eat, or sleep, or rule, for longing. His messengers searched the world for a year and came home with nothing. Then they followed the dream itself, up the river and over the mountain, across the sea to the Island of Britain, and through Eryri to Aber Seint in Arfon. There was the fort, the hall, the youths at their game, the old man at his carving, and the maiden on her golden chair. Her name was Elen.",
   "## Elen of the Hosts",
   "Macsen came to Britain and married her. For her bride gift Elen asked for the island for her father, and three great forts, at Arfon, Caerleon and Carmarthen. Then she had roads built from fort to fort across the island, so that the hosts of Britain could move from one end of it to the other, and they are called the roads of Elen of the Hosts, Sarn Helen, to this day.",
   "When Macsen went back to win Rome, Elen’s brothers Cynan and Gadeon went with him with the fighting men of Britain, and it was their skill that took the city. Afterwards Cynan and his men did not come home. They settled in Armorica, and it has been Llydaw, Brittany, ever since."],
  pCy=["Macsen Wledig oedd ymerawdwr Rhufain. Un diwrnod poeth, wrth hela, gorweddodd ar lan afon i gysgu, a breuddwydiodd. Teithiodd i fyny’r afon at y mynydd uchaf yn y byd, croesi drosto, a dod i lawr trwy wlad fawr at aber afon lle’r oedd llynges yn disgwyl. Hwyliodd i’r decaf o’r ynysoedd, ei chroesi o fôr i fôr, a dod at gaer ar aber afon yn wynebu ynys arall dros y dŵr.",
   "Yn y gaer roedd neuadd a tho aur arni. Eisteddai dau lanc yn chwarae gwyddbwyll ar glawr arian â gwerin aur. Eisteddai gŵr penwyn mewn cadair ifori yn naddu gwerin newydd. Ac mewn cadair o ruddaur eisteddai morwyn mor brydferth fel nad haws edrych arni nag ar yr haul. Cymerodd Macsen hi yn ei freichiau, ac yna cyfarthodd y cŵn a chlecian y gwaywffyn, a deffrôdd.",
   "Ni allai fwyta, na chysgu, na llywodraethu, gan hiraeth. Chwiliodd ei negeswyr y byd am flwyddyn a dod adref heb ddim. Yna dilynasant y freuddwyd ei hun, i fyny’r afon a thros y mynydd, dros y môr i Ynys Prydain, a thrwy Eryri i Aber Seint yn Arfon. Yno roedd y gaer, y neuadd, y llanciau wrth eu chwarae, yr hen ŵr wrth ei naddu, a’r forwyn yn ei chadair aur. Elen oedd ei henw.",
   "## Elen Luyddog",
   "Daeth Macsen i Brydain a’i phriodi. Yn rhodd briodas gofynnodd Elen am yr ynys i’w thad, a thair caer fawr, yn Arfon, Caerllion a Chaerfyrddin. Yna parodd wneud ffyrdd o gaer i gaer ar draws yr ynys, fel y gallai lluoedd Prydain symud o’r naill ben iddi i’r llall, a ffyrdd Elen Luyddog, Sarn Helen, y’u gelwir hyd heddiw.",
   "Pan aeth Macsen yn ôl i ennill Rhufain, aeth brodyr Elen, Cynan a Gadeon, gydag ef â rhyfelwyr Prydain, a’u medr hwy a enillodd y ddinas. Wedyn nid aeth Cynan a’i wŷr adref. Ymsefydlasant yn Armorica, a Llydaw yw hi byth ers hynny."],
  hEn="Macsen Wledig is Magnus Maximus, the commander of the Roman army in Britain, who was proclaimed emperor in 383 and ruled the West for five years. The tale’s fort at Aber Seint is Segontium, the Roman fort above Caernarfon, and the old Roman road that runs the length of Wales is still called Sarn Helen. Welsh kings were proud to trace their line to Macsen: the Pillar of Eliseg near Llangollen, carved in the ninth century, names Maximus among the forefathers of the kings of Powys. Brittany still speaks Breton, the sister of Cymraeg, and in 1983 Dafydd Iwan began Yma o Hyd with Macsen Wledig leaving Wales.",
  hCy="Macsen Wledig yw Magnus Maximus, cadlywydd byddin Rhufain ym Mhrydain, a gyhoeddwyd yn ymerawdwr yn 383 ac a reolodd y Gorllewin am bum mlynedd. Caer y chwedl yn Aber Seint yw Segontium, y gaer Rufeinig uwchben Caernarfon, a Sarn Helen y gelwir o hyd yr hen ffordd Rufeinig sy’n rhedeg ar hyd Cymru. Roedd brenhinoedd Cymru’n falch o olrhain eu llinach i Facsen: mae Piler Eliseg ger Llangollen, a gerfiwyd yn y nawfed ganrif, yn enwi Maximus ymhlith cyndadau brenhinoedd Powys. Mae Llydaw’n dal i siarad Llydaweg, chwaer y Gymraeg, ac yn 1983 dechreuodd Dafydd Iwan Yma o Hyd â Macsen Wledig yn gadael Cymru."),

 dict(id="lludd", era="ancient", art="plague", place="Llundain · Dinas Emrys",
  en="Lludd and Llefelys", cy="Cyfranc Lludd a Llefelys",
  sumEn="Three plagues fall on the Island of Britain, and two royal brothers outwit them all. This is how the dragons came to Dinas Emrys.",
  sumCy="Mae tair gormes yn disgyn ar Ynys Prydain, ac mae dau frawd brenhinol yn eu trechu i gyd. Dyma sut y daeth y dreigiau i Ddinas Emrys.",
  pEn=["Lludd son of Beli was king of the Island of Britain. He rebuilt the walls of London, and the city was called Caer Lludd after him. His brother Llefelys was king of France, and the two loved each other dearly. In Lludd’s time three plagues fell on the island.",
   "The first was a people called the Coraniaid, who could hear every word spoken anywhere in the island if the wind carried it to them, so that no plan could be made against them. The second was a scream heard every May Eve over every hearth in Britain, so terrible that the strong lost their strength, and the land and the waters were left barren. The third was that no food laid up in the king’s court lasted more than one night, however much there was, and no one knew where it went.",
   "Lludd sailed to France to ask his brother’s counsel. So that the Coraniaid could not hear, they spoke through a long horn of bronze. Even so, a spirit got into the horn and turned their kind words into hard ones, until they washed the horn out with wine and drove it away.",
   "## The answers",
   "Llefelys gave his brother some insects to crush in water. The water would kill the Coraniaid and harm no one of the island. Lludd called all the people together and sprinkled them, and the Coraniaid died.",
   "‘The scream,’ said Llefelys, ‘is the dragon of your island fighting a foreign dragon that is trying to overcome it.’ Lludd measured the island and found its very centre. There he dug a pit, set in it a vat of the best mead and covered it with a sheet of silk, and kept watch. The dragons came and fought in the air, and fell exhausted onto the silk as two little pigs, and drank the mead, and slept. Lludd wrapped them in the silk, shut them in a chest of stone, and buried them in the safest place in his kingdom: Dinas Emrys in Eryri. The scream was never heard again.",
   "The thief of the court was a mighty magician who lulled everyone to sleep with music. Lludd kept watch with a tub of cold water beside him, and whenever sleep came over him he stepped into it. At midnight the magician came with his great hamper, and Lludd fought him, and won, and made him his loyal man. So the island was freed of all three plagues."],
  pCy=["Lludd fab Beli oedd brenin Ynys Prydain. Ailgododd furiau Llundain, a galwyd y ddinas yn Gaer Ludd ar ei ôl. Ei frawd Llefelys oedd brenin Ffrainc, ac roedd y ddau’n caru’i gilydd yn fawr. Yn amser Lludd syrthiodd tair gormes ar yr ynys.",
   "Y gyntaf oedd pobl a elwid y Coraniaid, a glywai bob gair a leferid yn unrhyw le yn yr ynys os cludai’r gwynt ef atynt, fel na ellid gwneud dim cynllun yn eu herbyn. Yr ail oedd sgrech a glywid bob Nos Galan Mai uwch pob aelwyd ym Mhrydain, mor ofnadwy nes i’r cryfion golli eu nerth, a gadael y tir a’r dyfroedd yn ddiffrwyth. Y drydedd oedd na pharhâi dim bwyd a gedwid yn llys y brenin fwy nag un noson, er cymaint ohono oedd, ac ni wyddai neb i ble yr âi.",
   "Hwyliodd Lludd i Ffrainc i ofyn cyngor ei frawd. Rhag i’r Coraniaid glywed, siaradent trwy gorn hir o efydd. Er hynny aeth ysbryd i mewn i’r corn a throi eu geiriau caredig yn rhai caled, nes iddynt olchi’r corn â gwin a’i yrru allan.",
   "## Yr atebion",
   "Rhoddodd Llefelys bryfed i’w frawd i’w malu mewn dŵr. Lladdai’r dŵr y Coraniaid heb niweidio neb o’r ynys. Galwodd Lludd yr holl bobl ynghyd a’u taenellu, a bu farw’r Coraniaid.",
   "‘Y sgrech,’ meddai Llefelys, ‘yw draig dy ynys di’n ymladd â draig estron sy’n ceisio’i gorchfygu.’ Mesurodd Lludd yr ynys a chael ei chanol yn union. Yno cloddiodd bwll, gosod ynddo gerwyn o’r medd gorau a’i gorchuddio â llen o sidan, a chadw gwyliadwriaeth. Daeth y dreigiau ac ymladd yn yr awyr, a syrthio’n lluddedig ar y sidan yn ddau borchell, ac yfed y medd, a chysgu. Lapiodd Lludd hwy yn y sidan, eu cau mewn cist faen, a’u claddu yn y lle diogelaf yn ei deyrnas: Dinas Emrys yn Eryri. Ni chlywyd y sgrech byth wedyn.",
   "Lleidr y llys oedd swynwr nerthol a suai bawb i gysgu â cherddoriaeth. Cadwodd Lludd wyliadwriaeth â thwba o ddŵr oer wrth ei ymyl, a phryd bynnag y deuai cwsg drosto camai i mewn iddo. Am hanner nos daeth y swynwr â’i gawell mawr, ac ymladdodd Lludd ag ef, a’i drechu, a’i wneud yn ŵr ffyddlon iddo. Felly y rhyddhawyd yr ynys o’r tair gormes."],
  hEn="This tale was written down by about 1200 and survives in the White Book of Rhydderch and the Red Book of Hergest; a version of it was also set into the Welsh Brut, the history of the kings of Britain. It explains why the red dragon was lying under Dinas Emrys when the boy Emrys told Gwrtheyrn where to dig, so the two legends join into one story. Lludd is remembered in London’s Ludgate. His name goes back to Nodens, a British god whose great temple stood at Lydney above the Severn in Roman times, and Welsh tradition remembers him too as Nudd, father of Gwyn ap Nudd, king of the fair folk.",
  hCy="Ysgrifennwyd y chwedl hon erbyn tua 1200 ac mae wedi goroesi yn Llyfr Gwyn Rhydderch a Llyfr Coch Hergest; rhoddwyd fersiwn ohoni hefyd yn y Brut Cymraeg, hanes brenhinoedd Prydain. Mae’n egluro pam roedd y ddraig goch yn gorwedd dan Ddinas Emrys pan ddywedodd y bachgen Emrys wrth Wrtheyrn ble i gloddio, felly mae’r ddwy chwedl yn uno’n un stori. Cofir Lludd yn Ludgate yn Llundain. Mae ei enw’n mynd yn ôl at Nodens, duw Brythonig yr oedd ei deml fawr yn Lydney uwchben Hafren yn oes y Rhufeiniaid, ac mae traddodiad Cymru’n ei gofio hefyd fel Nudd, tad Gwyn ap Nudd, brenin y tylwyth teg."),

 dict(id="dwynwen", era="saints", art="island", place="Ynys Llanddwyn, Ynys Môn",
  en="Dwynwen, saint of lovers", cy="Santes Dwynwen",
  sumEn="A princess who could not have the man she loved asks for three wishes, and gives all of them away.",
  sumCy="Mae tywysoges na allai gael y gŵr a garai yn gofyn am dri dymuniad, ac yn rhoi’r tri i eraill.",
  pEn=["Dwynwen was one of the many daughters of Brychan, king of Brycheiniog, and the loveliest of them all. A young man named Maelon fell in love with her, and she with him. But it was not to be, and the parting left them both in such grief that Dwynwen went alone into the forest and prayed to be freed from her sorrow.",
   "As she slept, an angel came to her with a cup of sweet drink. When she drank it, she was healed of her grief. But in her dream she saw that Maelon had been given the same drink and had been turned to a block of ice.",
   "Then she was granted three wishes. With the first she asked that Maelon be thawed and made whole. With the second she asked that God would hear the prayers of all true lovers who called on her. With the third, that she herself would never wish to marry. All three were granted.",
   "Dwynwen gave her life to God. She crossed to a little tidal island off the west coast of Ynys Môn, Llanddwyn, and built a cell there, and lived out her days looking over the sea to the mountains of Eryri. Lovers came to her well for centuries. It was said that the fish that swam in it could tell whether a couple’s love would be happy, by the way they moved in the water."],
  pCy=["Roedd Dwynwen yn un o ferched niferus Brychan, brenin Brycheiniog, a’r harddaf ohonynt i gyd. Syrthiodd llanc o’r enw Maelon mewn cariad â hi, a hithau ag yntau. Ond nid felly y bu, a gadawodd yr ymadael y ddau mewn cymaint galar nes i Ddwynwen fynd ar ei phen ei hun i’r goedwig a gweddïo am gael ei rhyddhau o’i thristwch.",
   "Wrth iddi gysgu daeth angel ati â chwpan o ddiod felys. Pan yfodd hi, fe’i hiachawyd o’i galar. Ond yn ei breuddwyd gwelodd fod Maelon wedi cael yr un ddiod ac wedi’i droi’n dalp o rew.",
   "Yna rhoddwyd iddi dri dymuniad. Â’r cyntaf gofynnodd am i Faelon gael ei ddadmer a’i adfer. Â’r ail gofynnodd am i Dduw wrando ar weddïau pob gwir gariad a alwai arni hi. Â’r trydydd, na fynnai hi ei hun briodi byth. Caniatawyd y tri.",
   "Rhoddodd Dwynwen ei bywyd i Dduw. Croesodd i ynys lanw fach oddi ar arfordir gorllewinol Môn, Llanddwyn, a chodi cell yno, a byw weddill ei dyddiau’n edrych dros y môr at fynyddoedd Eryri. Daeth cariadon at ei ffynnon am ganrifoedd. Dywedid y gallai’r pysgod a nofiai ynddi ddweud a fyddai cariad dau’n hapus, wrth y ffordd y symudent yn y dŵr."],
  hEn="Dwynwen lived in the fifth century, and her feast day, 25 January, is Dydd Santes Dwynwen, the Welsh day of love, when cards and gifts are exchanged across Wales. Her church on Llanddwyn grew into one of the richest shrines in Wales in the Middle Ages, and its ruins still stand among the dunes beside a tall stone cross and the old pilots’ lighthouse. Dafydd ap Gwilym, the greatest poet of medieval Wales, wrote a cywydd to Dwynwen asking her to carry a message of love for him. Llanddwyn is now part of a national nature reserve.",
  hCy="Roedd Dwynwen yn byw yn y bumed ganrif, a’i gŵyl, 25 Ionawr, yw Dydd Santes Dwynwen, dydd y cariadon yng Nghymru, pan anfonir cardiau ac anrhegion ar draws y wlad. Tyfodd ei heglwys ar Landdwyn yn un o gysegrfannau cyfoethocaf Cymru yn yr Oesoedd Canol, ac mae ei hadfeilion yn dal i sefyll ymhlith y twyni ger croes garreg uchel a hen oleudy’r peilotiaid. Ysgrifennodd Dafydd ap Gwilym, bardd mwyaf Cymru’r Oesoedd Canol, gywydd i Ddwynwen yn gofyn iddi gludo neges serch drosto. Mae Llanddwyn heddiw’n rhan o warchodfa natur genedlaethol."),

 dict(id="melangell", era="saints", art="hare", place="Pennant Melangell, Powys",
  en="Melangell and the hare", cy="Melangell a’r ysgyfarnog",
  sumEn="A hunted hare hides under a holy woman’s cloak, and a prince of Powys gives her the valley for ever.",
  sumCy="Mae ysgyfarnog dan helfa’n cuddio dan fantell gwraig sanctaidd, ac mae tywysog Powys yn rhoi’r cwm iddi am byth.",
  pEn=["Melangell was the daughter of a king in Ireland. Her father wished her to marry a nobleman of his court, but she had given her heart to God, and she crossed the sea in secret and came to the head of a lonely valley under the Berwyn mountains. There she lived alone for fifteen years, praying, and sleeping on a bare rock.",
   "One day Brochwel Ysgithrog, prince of Powys, came hunting in the valley. His hounds started a hare and chased it into a thicket. There Brochwel found a young woman deep in prayer, and the hare crouched under the hem of her cloak, facing the hounds boldly. He urged the hounds on, but they drew back howling and would not go near. His huntsman lifted his horn to blow, and the horn stuck to his lips.",
   "Brochwel asked her who she was, and she told him her story. Moved, he gave her the valley as a sanctuary for ever, where any man or beast that fled there would be safe. Melangell lived there for thirty-seven years more. Women came to join her, and the valley became a house of prayer.",
   "After her death the hares of the valley were called ŵyn bach Melangell, Melangell’s little lambs, and for centuries no one in the parish would kill a hare. It was said that if anyone called out ‘God and Melangell be with you!’ to a hunted hare, it would surely escape."],
  pCy=["Merch i frenin yn Iwerddon oedd Melangell. Mynnai ei thad iddi briodi uchelwr o’i lys, ond roedd hi wedi rhoi ei chalon i Dduw, a chroesodd y môr yn ddirgel a dod i ben draw cwm unig dan fynyddoedd y Berwyn. Yno bu’n byw ar ei phen ei hun am bymtheng mlynedd, yn gweddïo, ac yn cysgu ar graig noeth.",
   "Un diwrnod daeth Brochwel Ysgithrog, tywysog Powys, i hela yn y cwm. Cododd ei gŵn ysgyfarnog a’i hymlid i ddrysni. Yno cafodd Brochwel ferch ifanc yn ddwys mewn gweddi, a’r ysgyfarnog yn swatio dan odre’i mantell, yn wynebu’r cŵn yn hy. Anogodd y cŵn ymlaen, ond ciliasant gan udo ac ni fynnent fynd yn agos. Cododd ei heliwr ei gorn i’w ganu, a glynodd y corn wrth ei wefusau.",
   "Gofynnodd Brochwel iddi pwy oedd hi, ac adroddodd ei hanes. Wedi’i gyffwrdd, rhoddodd y cwm iddi’n noddfa am byth, lle byddai unrhyw ddyn neu anifail a ffoai yno’n ddiogel. Bu Melangell fyw yno am ddwy flynedd ar bymtheg ar hugain eto. Daeth gwragedd i ymuno â hi, a daeth y cwm yn dŷ gweddi.",
   "Wedi ei marw gelwid ysgyfarnogod y cwm yn ŵyn bach Melangell, ac am ganrifoedd ni laddai neb yn y plwyf ysgyfarnog. Dywedid pe gwaeddai rhywun ‘Duw a Melangell a’th gadwo!’ ar ysgyfarnog dan helfa, y dihangai’n siŵr."],
  hEn="Melangell lived in the seventh century, and Brochwel Ysgithrog was a real prince of Powys. Her church at Pennant Melangell, at the end of a road in the Tanat valley, still holds her shrine: a carved stone reliquary of the twelfth century, rebuilt from its scattered pieces in the 1950s and again in the 1990s, and the oldest Romanesque shrine of its kind in northern Europe. A wooden screen of the fifteenth century in the church shows the whole story, the prince, the hounds, the huntsman and the hare. Her feast day is 27 May, and she is the patron saint of hares and of all creatures in need of shelter.",
  hCy="Roedd Melangell yn byw yn y seithfed ganrif, ac roedd Brochwel Ysgithrog yn dywysog go iawn ym Mhowys. Mae ei heglwys ym Mhennant Melangell, ym mhen draw ffordd yn nyffryn Tanad, yn dal i gadw ei chysegrfa: creirfa faen gerfiedig o’r ddeuddegfed ganrif, a ailgodwyd o’i darnau gwasgaredig yn y 1950au ac eto yn y 1990au, a’r gysegrfa Romanésg hynaf o’i bath yng ngogledd Ewrop. Mae sgrin bren o’r bymthegfed ganrif yn yr eglwys yn dangos yr holl stori, y tywysog, y cŵn, yr heliwr a’r ysgyfarnog. Ei gŵyl yw 27 Mai, a hi yw nawddsant ysgyfarnogod a phob creadur sydd angen lloches."),

 dict(id="tylwyth", era="medieval", art="fairy", place="Abertawe · Swansea",
  en="Elidyr and the Tylwyth Teg", cy="Elidyr a’r Tylwyth Teg",
  sumEn="A boy runs away from his lessons and is led underground to the country of the fair folk.",
  sumCy="Mae bachgen yn rhedeg i ffwrdd o’i wersi ac yn cael ei arwain dan ddaear i wlad y tylwyth teg.",
  pEn=["When Elidyr was twelve years old and learning his letters, he ran away from his lessons and hid in a hollow under the bank of a river. He had been there two days without food when two little men appeared and said, ‘Come with us, and we will take you to a land full of games and delights.’",
   "They led him along a dark path underground, and out into a beautiful country of rivers and meadows and woods. But it was a dim country, for the sun never shone there, and the nights were black with no moon or stars. The people were very small, with long fair hair that fell over their shoulders, and they rode little horses no bigger than greyhounds. They ate no meat or fish, but lived on milk, made into dishes flavoured with saffron. They never swore an oath, and they hated a lie more than anything.",
   "Elidyr was brought before their king, and he played with the king’s son, and he learned their language. Often he went back up the path to visit his mother, and told her everything. One day she asked him to bring her some of their gold. He took a golden ball that the king’s son played with, and ran with it up the path to his mother’s house. Two of the little people ran after him, and at her doorway he tripped. They snatched the ball from his hand and were gone, with looks of scorn for him.",
   "He never found the path again. For a whole year he searched the river bank for the opening, but it was gone. Elidyr grew up, and became a priest, and lived to be an old man. But whenever he told the story, he wept."],
  pCy=["Pan oedd Elidyr yn ddeuddeg oed ac yn dysgu ei lythrennau, rhedodd i ffwrdd o’i wersi a chuddio mewn pant dan lan afon. Bu yno ddeuddydd heb fwyd pan ymddangosodd dau ddyn bach a dweud, ‘Tyrd gyda ni, ac fe awn â thi i wlad yn llawn chwaraeon a hyfrydwch.’",
   "Arweiniasant ef ar hyd llwybr tywyll dan ddaear, ac allan i wlad hardd o afonydd a dolydd a choedydd. Ond gwlad dywyll oedd hi, oherwydd ni thywynnai’r haul yno byth, ac roedd y nosweithiau’n ddu heb leuad na sêr. Roedd y bobl yn fach iawn, â gwallt golau hir dros eu hysgwyddau, a marchogent geffylau bychain heb fod yn fwy na milgwn. Ni fwytaent na chig na physgod, ond byw ar laeth, wedi’i wneud yn seigiau â blas saffrwm arnynt. Ni thyngent lw byth, ac roedd yn gas ganddynt gelwydd yn fwy na dim.",
   "Dygwyd Elidyr gerbron eu brenin, a chwaraeodd gyda mab y brenin, a dysgu eu hiaith. Yn aml âi yn ôl i fyny’r llwybr i ymweld â’i fam, a dweud y cwbl wrthi. Un diwrnod gofynnodd hi iddo ddod â pheth o’u haur iddi. Cymerodd bêl aur y chwaraeai mab y brenin â hi, a rhedeg â hi i fyny’r llwybr i dŷ ei fam. Rhedodd dau o’r bobl fach ar ei ôl, ac wrth ddrws ei fam baglodd. Cipiasant y bêl o’i law a diflannu, gan edrych arno’n ddirmygus.",
   "Ni chafodd hyd i’r llwybr byth wedyn. Am flwyddyn gyfan chwiliodd lan yr afon am yr agoriad, ond roedd wedi mynd. Tyfodd Elidyr yn ddyn, a daeth yn offeiriad, a byw i fod yn hen ŵr. Ond pryd bynnag yr adroddai’r stori, wylai."],
  hEn="This is the oldest written tale of the Tylwyth Teg, the ‘fair family’, as the Welsh politely called the fairy folk. Gerald of Wales, Gerallt Gymro, set it down in his Journey through Wales after travelling the country in 1188. He heard it near Swansea, and wrote that Elidyr told it as an old man to David, bishop of St Davids. Gerald was struck that the words Elidyr remembered from the fairy tongue were close to Greek: their word for water was ‘udor’, Greek ‘hydor’. Tales of the Tylwyth Teg were told in every corner of Wales into the twentieth century, of fairy rings, of lake maidens, and of Plant Rhys Ddwfn, whose green islands lie hidden off the coast of Pembrokeshire.",
  hCy="Dyma’r chwedl ysgrifenedig hynaf am y Tylwyth Teg, fel y galwai’r Cymry’r bobl fach yn gwrtais. Rhoddodd Gerallt Gymro hi ar glawr yn ei Hanes y Daith trwy Gymru, wedi iddo deithio’r wlad yn 1188. Clywodd hi ger Abertawe, ac ysgrifennodd i Elidyr ei hadrodd yn hen ŵr wrth Dafydd, esgob Tyddewi. Trawyd Gerallt fod y geiriau a gofiai Elidyr o iaith y tylwyth teg yn agos at Roeg: eu gair am ddŵr oedd ‘udor’, ‘hydor’ yn Roeg. Adroddid straeon am y Tylwyth Teg ym mhob cwr o Gymru hyd at yr ugeinfed ganrif, am gylchoedd y tylwyth teg, am forynion y llynnoedd, ac am Blant Rhys Ddwfn, y mae eu hynysoedd gwyrdd ynghudd oddi ar arfordir Sir Benfro."),

 dict(id="afanc", era="ancient", art="afanc", place="Betws-y-Coed · Glaslyn, Eryri",
  en="The Afanc of Llyn yr Afanc", cy="Afanc Llyn yr Afanc",
  sumEn="A monster in the river Conwy floods the valley, until a brave girl sings it to sleep and two great oxen drag it over the mountains.",
  sumCy="Mae anghenfil yn afon Conwy’n boddi’r dyffryn, nes i ferch ddewr ei suo i gysgu ac i ddau ych mawr ei lusgo dros y mynyddoedd.",
  pEn=["In a deep pool of the river Conwy, a little above Betws-y-Coed, there lived an afanc: a huge, scaly water-beast, older than anyone could remember. Whenever it stirred and thrashed, the river burst its banks and drowned the fields of the valley. Spears bounced off its hide. No one could kill it, so the people of the valley decided to move it somewhere it could do no harm.",
   "First it had to be brought out of the water. A young woman of the valley, braver than any of the men, went down to the edge of the pool and sang. The afanc rose to listen. It came up onto the bank, and laid its great head in her lap, and fell asleep. Quietly the men wound iron chains around it.",
   "The afanc woke and plunged back towards the pool, and the chains began to slip. So the people brought the Ychen Bannog, the great long-horned oxen of Hu Gadarn, the strongest beasts in the land. Hitched to the chains, they dragged the afanc out of the river and up into the mountains, through the passes of Eryri. On one steep slope one of the oxen pulled so hard that its eye burst from its head, and the place has been called Pwll Llygad yr Ych, the Pool of the Ox’s Eye, ever since.",
   "At last they came to Glaslyn, the deep blue lake high under the summit of Yr Wyddfa, and let the afanc down into it. The water was too deep and the mountain walls too steep for it to climb out, and it lies there still. The valley of the Conwy was never flooded by it again."],
  pCy=["Mewn pwll dwfn yn afon Conwy, ychydig uwchlaw Betws-y-Coed, roedd afanc yn byw: bwystfil dŵr anferth, cennog, hŷn nag y gallai neb gofio. Pryd bynnag y cyffroai ac y trawai yn y dŵr, torrai’r afon dros ei glannau a boddi caeau’r dyffryn. Neidiai’r gwaywffyn oddi ar ei groen. Ni allai neb ei ladd, felly penderfynodd pobl y dyffryn ei symud i rywle lle na allai wneud niwed.",
   "Yn gyntaf rhaid oedd ei ddwyn o’r dŵr. Aeth merch ifanc o’r dyffryn, dewrach na’r un o’r dynion, i lawr at ymyl y pwll a chanu. Cododd yr afanc i wrando. Daeth i fyny ar y lan, a gosod ei ben mawr yn ei harffed, a chysgu. Yn dawel lapiodd y dynion gadwyni haearn amdano.",
   "Deffrôdd yr afanc a rhuthro’n ôl tua’r pwll, a dechreuodd y cadwyni lithro. Felly daeth y bobl â’r Ychen Bannog, ychen corniog mawr Hu Gadarn, yr anifeiliaid cryfaf yn y wlad. Wedi’u bachu wrth y cadwyni, llusgasant yr afanc o’r afon ac i fyny i’r mynyddoedd, trwy fylchau Eryri. Ar un llethr serth tynnodd un o’r ychen mor galed nes i’w lygad neidio o’i ben, a Phwll Llygad yr Ych y gelwir y lle byth ers hynny.",
   "O’r diwedd daethant at Lyn Glas, y llyn dwfn glas yn uchel dan gopa’r Wyddfa, a gollwng yr afanc iddo. Roedd y dŵr yn rhy ddwfn a muriau’r mynydd yn rhy serth iddo ddringo allan, ac mae yno o hyd. Ni foddwyd dyffryn Conwy ganddo byth wedyn."],
  hEn="The tale was told for centuries in the Conwy valley, and the Welsh Triads count the drawing of the afanc out of the lake by the oxen of Hu Gadarn among the three great feats of the Island of Britain. In modern Welsh ‘afanc’ is the word for a beaver. Beavers really did live in Welsh rivers: Gerald of Wales watched them building their lodges on the Teifi in 1188 and said it was the only river in Wales or England where they could still be found. Hunted out in the centuries after, they are now being brought back to Wales once more.",
  hCy="Adroddwyd y chwedl am ganrifoedd yn nyffryn Conwy, ac mae’r Trioedd yn cyfrif llusgo’r afanc o’r llyn gan ychen Hu Gadarn ymhlith tri champ mawr Ynys Prydain. Mewn Cymraeg modern ‘afanc’ yw’r gair am beaver. Roedd afancod yn byw go iawn yn afonydd Cymru: gwyliodd Gerallt Gymro hwy’n codi eu llochesau ar afon Teifi yn 1188, a dweud mai honno oedd yr unig afon yng Nghymru neu Loegr lle ceid hwy o hyd. Wedi’u hela i ddiflannu yn y canrifoedd wedyn, maent bellach yn cael eu dwyn yn ôl i Gymru unwaith eto."),

 dict(id="cadair", era="kings", art="chair", place="Cadair Idris, Meirionnydd",
  en="The chair of Idris", cy="Cadair Idris",
  sumEn="A giant poet sat on this mountain to read the stars. Spend a night on it, and you come down a poet, or mad.",
  sumCy="Eisteddai cawr o fardd ar y mynydd hwn i ddarllen y sêr. Treuliwch noson arno, a dewch i lawr yn fardd, neu’n wallgof.",
  pEn=["Long ago the giant Idris lived among the mountains of Meirionnydd. He was a giant of the mind as well as the body: a poet, a philosopher and a watcher of the stars. On clear nights he would climb to the high ridge above the sea and sit in a great hollow of rock, his chair, to read the heavens.",
   "Once, as he walked down the mountain, he felt three pebbles in his shoe. He shook them out, and they were three great boulders, which lie to this day beside a little lake on the slopes, called Llyn y Tri Greyenyn, the Lake of the Three Pebbles.",
   "Below the summit is Llyn Cau, dark and deep in its hollow under the cliffs. It is said to have no bottom, and that a monster lives in it which once took a young swimmer down. And on stormy nights, they say, the hounds of Annwn run over the ridge, white with red ears, hunting souls with Gwyn ap Nudd.",
   "But the best known saying of the mountain is this. Whoever spends a night alone on the summit of Cadair Idris will come down in the morning either a poet, or a madman. Many have tried it, to see which."],
  pCy=["Amser maith yn ôl roedd y cawr Idris yn byw ymhlith mynyddoedd Meirionnydd. Roedd yn gawr o feddwl yn ogystal ag o gorff: yn fardd, yn athronydd ac yn wyliwr y sêr. Ar nosweithiau clir dringai i’r grib uchel uwchlaw’r môr ac eistedd mewn pant mawr o graig, ei gadair, i ddarllen y nefoedd.",
   "Unwaith, wrth gerdded i lawr y mynydd, teimlodd dair carreg fach yn ei esgid. Ysgydwodd hwy allan, a thri chlogfaen mawr oeddent, sy’n gorwedd hyd heddiw ger llyn bach ar y llethrau, a elwir Llyn y Tri Greyenyn.",
   "Dan y copa mae Llyn Cau, yn dywyll a dwfn yn ei bant dan y clogwyni. Dywedir nad oes gwaelod iddo, a bod anghenfil yn byw ynddo a dynnodd nofiwr ifanc i lawr unwaith. Ac ar nosweithiau stormus, meddir, mae Cŵn Annwn yn rhedeg dros y grib, yn wyn â chlustiau cochion, yn hela eneidiau gyda Gwyn ap Nudd.",
   "Ond dyma ddywediad enwocaf y mynydd. Pwy bynnag a dreulia noson ar ei ben ei hun ar gopa Cadair Idris, fe ddaw i lawr yn y bore naill ai’n fardd, neu’n wallgof. Mae llawer wedi rhoi cynnig arni, i weld pa un."],
  hEn="Welsh tradition links the mountain’s name with Idris ap Gwyddno, a real king of Meirionnydd who fell in battle on the Severn in 632. Its summit, Pen y Gadair, rises to 893 metres, and its great cirques were carved by ice. The saying about the poet and the madman was famous across Britain by the 1800s: Felicia Hemans, who grew up in north Wales, wrote ‘The Rock of Cader Idris’ about a night on the summit, and the mountain drew painters such as Richard Wilson, the father of British landscape painting, born at Penegoes nearby.",
  hCy="Mae traddodiad Cymru’n cysylltu enw’r mynydd ag Idris ap Gwyddno, brenin go iawn ar Feirionnydd a syrthiodd mewn brwydr ar Hafren yn 632. Mae ei gopa, Pen y Gadair, yn codi i 893 metr, a naddwyd ei bantiau mawr gan rew. Roedd y dywediad am y bardd a’r gwallgof yn enwog ar draws Prydain erbyn y 1800au: ysgrifennodd Felicia Hemans, a fagwyd yng ngogledd Cymru, ‘The Rock of Cader Idris’ am noson ar y copa, a denodd y mynydd arlunwyr fel Richard Wilson, tad peintio tirluniau Prydain, a aned ym Mhenegoes gerllaw."),

 dict(id="owain", era="medieval", art="cave", place="Moel Hebog · Glyn y Groes",
  en="Owain Glyndŵr, who never died", cy="Owain Glyndŵr, na fu farw",
  sumEn="The last native Prince of Wales was never captured. Where did he go?",
  sumCy="Ni ddaliwyd tywysog brodorol olaf Cymru erioed. I ble yr aeth?",
  pEn=["When the great rising was at its height, Owain Glyndŵr was hunted through the mountains of Eryri. Once, it is told, his enemies came on him near Beddgelert, and he escaped them by climbing the sheer gully on the face of Moel Hebog that no one else dared to climb. On the far side of the mountain he hid in a cave in the cliff for months, and the prior of Beddgelert brought him food. The cave is still called Ogof Owain Glyndŵr.",
   "Years later, when the war was over, Owain was walking alone at dawn in the Berwyn hills and met the abbot of Glyn y Groes. ‘You are up early, abbot,’ said Owain. ‘No,’ said the abbot, ‘it is you who have risen too early, by a hundred years.’ Owain understood him, and walked on into the mist, and the abbot never saw him again. Before the century was out, a man of Welsh blood, Harri Tudur, took the crown of England under the red dragon.",
   "## Asleep in the mountain",
   "Owain was never captured and never betrayed. The king offered him a pardon, twice, and he never took it. And no one ever found his grave. So the people of Wales said that he had not died at all. He and his men, they said, lie asleep in a cave in the mountains, in full armour, and when Wales has need of him again Owain will wake, and come out, and lead his people."],
  pCy=["Pan oedd y gwrthryfel mawr ar ei anterth, erlidiwyd Owain Glyndŵr trwy fynyddoedd Eryri. Unwaith, meddir, daeth ei elynion arno ger Beddgelert, a dihangodd rhagddynt drwy ddringo’r hollt serth ar wyneb Moel Hebog na feiddiai neb arall ei dringo. Yr ochr draw i’r mynydd cuddiodd mewn ogof yn y clogwyn am fisoedd, a dygai prior Beddgelert fwyd iddo. Ogof Owain Glyndŵr y gelwir hi o hyd.",
   "Flynyddoedd wedyn, wedi i’r rhyfel ddod i ben, roedd Owain yn cerdded ar ei ben ei hun ar doriad gwawr ym mryniau’r Berwyn a chyfarfu ag abad Glyn y Groes. ‘Rydych yn codi’n fore, abad,’ meddai Owain. ‘Na,’ meddai’r abad, ‘chi a gododd yn rhy fore, o gan mlynedd.’ Deallodd Owain ef, a cherdded ymlaen i’r niwl, ac ni welodd yr abad ef byth wedyn. Cyn diwedd y ganrif, cymerodd gŵr o waed Cymreig, Harri Tudur, goron Lloegr dan y ddraig goch.",
   "## Ynghwsg yn y mynydd",
   "Ni ddaliwyd Owain erioed ac ni chafodd ei fradychu. Cynigiodd y brenin bardwn iddo, ddwywaith, ac ni dderbyniodd ef byth. Ac ni chafodd neb hyd i’w fedd. Felly dywedodd pobl Cymru nad oedd wedi marw o gwbl. Mae ef a’i wŷr, meddent, yn cysgu mewn ogof yn y mynyddoedd, yn eu harfwisg, a phan fydd ar Gymru ei angen eto, fe ddeffry Owain, a dod allan, ac arwain ei bobl."],
  hEn="Owain Glyndŵr was proclaimed Prince of Wales at Glyndyfrdwy on 16 September 1400, now Owain Glyndŵr Day, and for a time ruled most of Wales, calling parliaments at Machynlleth and Harlech and writing to the King of France from Pennal in 1406. After 1410 he disappears from the record. Pardons were offered to him in 1415 and 1416, and no answer ever came. Adam of Usk, writing at the time, recorded that Owain died in 1415 and was buried by night by his followers, so that his enemies could never find his grave. To this day no one knows where he lies, and no one ever gave him up.",
  hCy="Cyhoeddwyd Owain Glyndŵr yn Dywysog Cymru yng Nglyndyfrdwy ar 16 Medi 1400, sef Diwrnod Owain Glyndŵr bellach, ac am gyfnod rheolodd y rhan fwyaf o Gymru, gan alw seneddau ym Machynlleth a Harlech ac ysgrifennu at Frenin Ffrainc o Bennal yn 1406. Wedi 1410 mae’n diflannu o’r cofnodion. Cynigiwyd pardwn iddo yn 1415 ac 1416, ac ni ddaeth ateb byth. Cofnododd Adda o Frynbuga, a ysgrifennai ar y pryd, i Owain farw yn 1415 a chael ei gladdu liw nos gan ei ddilynwyr, fel na châi ei elynion fyth hyd i’w fedd. Hyd heddiw ni ŵyr neb ble mae’n gorwedd, ac ni fradychodd neb ef erioed."),

 dict(id="twm", era="tudor", art="outlaw", place="Tregaron · Rhandirmwyn",
  en="Twm Siôn Cati, the Welsh Robin Hood", cy="Twm Siôn Cati",
  sumEn="The cleverest man in Ceredigion outwits the greedy, hides in a cave above the Tywi, and ends up a respected squire.",
  sumCy="Mae dyn cyfrwysaf Ceredigion yn trechu’r barus, yn cuddio mewn ogof uwchben y Tywi, ac yn diweddu’n ŵr bonheddig uchel ei barch.",
  pEn=["Twm Siôn Cati grew up in Tregaron, the son of Catherine, and from a boy he was quicker than anyone in Ceredigion. The tales say he never robbed the poor. His tricks were played on the proud, the greedy and the cheating, and the ordinary people of the hills loved him for it.",
   "Once, at a fair, Twm picked up an iron pot from a dealer’s stall. ‘There is a hole in this,’ he said. ‘There is not,’ said the dealer. ‘There is,’ said Twm, ‘and I can prove it. Put your head inside and look.’ The dealer put the pot over his head and peered about. ‘There’s no hole at all,’ came his voice from inside. ‘Then how did you get your head in?’ said Twm. By the time the dealer had pulled it off, Twm and the pot were gone.",
   "When the sheriff’s men came after him, Twm would vanish into a cave high on a wooded crag above the river Tywi, near Rhandirmwyn, where only a man who knew the way could reach him. He was a master of disguise, and more than once he met the men hunting him on the road and sent them off in the wrong direction.",
   "In the end, the tales say, Twm won the heart of a rich heiress of the Tywi valley, received a full pardon, and settled down as a squire and a magistrate, as respected as he had once been hunted. He spent his later years as a learned man, writing out the family trees of the gentry of Wales."],
  pCy=["Yn Nhregaron y magwyd Twm Siôn Cati, mab Catrin, ac o’i blentyndod roedd yn sydynach na neb yng Ngheredigion. Dywed y chwedlau na ladratodd erioed oddi ar y tlawd. Chwaraeai ei driciau ar y balch, y barus a’r twyllwyr, a châi ei garu am hynny gan bobl gyffredin y bryniau.",
   "Unwaith, mewn ffair, cododd Twm grochan haearn o stondin masnachwr. ‘Mae twll yn hwn,’ meddai. ‘Nac oes,’ meddai’r masnachwr. ‘Oes,’ meddai Twm, ‘a gallaf ei brofi. Rhowch eich pen i mewn ac edrych.’ Rhoddodd y masnachwr y crochan dros ei ben a chraffu o’i gwmpas. ‘Does dim twll o gwbl,’ meddai ei lais o’r tu mewn. ‘Sut cawsoch chi eich pen i mewn, ’te?’ meddai Twm. Erbyn i’r masnachwr ei dynnu i ffwrdd, roedd Twm a’r crochan wedi mynd.",
   "Pan ddeuai gwŷr y siryf ar ei ôl, diflannai Twm i ogof yn uchel ar glogwyn coediog uwchben afon Tywi, ger Rhandirmwyn, lle na allai neb ond un a wyddai’r ffordd ei gyrraedd. Roedd yn feistr ar ymddieithrio, a mwy nag unwaith cyfarfu â’r dynion a’i heliai ar y ffordd a’u hanfon i’r cyfeiriad anghywir.",
   "Yn y diwedd, medd y chwedlau, enillodd Twm galon aeres gyfoethog o ddyffryn Tywi, cael pardwn llawn, ac ymsefydlu’n ŵr bonheddig ac yn ynad, yr un mor uchel ei barch ag y bu unwaith dan helfa. Treuliodd ei flynyddoedd olaf yn ŵr dysgedig, yn llunio achau boneddigion Cymru."],
  hEn="Twm Siôn Cati was a real man: Thomas Jones of Porth-y-ffynnon, Tregaron, who lived from about 1530 to 1609. He received a general pardon in 1559, and became one of the most respected genealogists and herald-bards of his day, and his writings on the family trees of Wales survive to this day. His cave, Ogof Twm Siôn Cati, can still be visited on the RSPB’s Dinas reserve near Rhandirmwyn. The tales of the trickster grew in the telling, and T. J. Llewelyn Prichard’s novel of 1828, The Adventures and Vagaries of Twm Shon Catti, made him the Robin Hood of Wales.",
  hCy="Roedd Twm Siôn Cati’n ddyn go iawn: Thomas Jones o Borth-y-ffynnon, Tregaron, a fu fyw o tua 1530 hyd 1609. Cafodd bardwn cyffredinol yn 1559, a daeth yn un o achyddion a beirdd herodrol uchaf eu parch ei ddydd, ac mae ei ysgrifau ar achau Cymru wedi goroesi hyd heddiw. Gellir ymweld ag Ogof Twm Siôn Cati o hyd ar warchodfa Dinas yr RSPB ger Rhandirmwyn. Tyfodd y straeon am y twyllwr wrth eu hadrodd, a gwnaeth nofel T. J. Llewelyn Prichard yn 1828, The Adventures and Vagaries of Twm Shon Catti, ef yn Robin Hood Cymru."),
 dict(id="gwenffrewi", era="saints", art="well", place="Treffynnon · Holywell",
  en="Gwenffrewi and the holy well", cy="Gwenffrewi a’r ffynnon sanctaidd",
  sumEn="Where a young saint fell, a spring burst out of the ground. Pilgrims have come to it for thirteen hundred years.",
  sumCy="Lle syrthiodd santes ifanc, tarddodd ffynnon o’r ddaear. Mae pererinion wedi dod ati ers mil a thri chant o flynyddoedd.",
  pEn=["Gwenffrewi was the daughter of a nobleman of Tegeingl in the north-east of Wales, and the niece of the great saint Beuno, who had built a church in the valley below her father’s house. She sat at his feet to hear him teach, and gave her life to God.",
   "One Sunday, when her family had gone down to church, a prince named Caradog came to the house and found her alone. He wanted her for himself. She would not have him, and she fled down the hill towards the church. He rode after her, caught her at the church door, and in his fury struck off her head with his sword.",
   "Where her head fell, a spring of clear water burst out of the ground, so strong that it ran down to the sea. Beuno came out of the church, took up her head, set it back on her shoulders, and prayed. Gwenffrewi opened her eyes and stood up, alive, with only a thin white line around her neck to show where the sword had passed. Caradog, it is said, sank into the earth and was never seen again.",
   "Gwenffrewi lived for many years after, and became abbess of a house of holy women at Gwytherin, in the hills above the Conwy. But the spring went on flowing where she fell, and the stones at the bottom of it were stained red, and the moss around it smelled sweet. People came from all over Wales to bathe in it, and many said they were healed."],
  pCy=["Merch i uchelwr o Degeingl yng ngogledd-ddwyrain Cymru oedd Gwenffrewi, a nith i’r sant mawr Beuno, a oedd wedi codi eglwys yn y cwm islaw tŷ ei thad. Eisteddai wrth ei draed i’w glywed yn dysgu, a rhoddodd ei bywyd i Dduw.",
   "Un Sul, pan oedd ei theulu wedi mynd i lawr i’r eglwys, daeth tywysog o’r enw Caradog at y tŷ a’i chael ar ei phen ei hun. Mynnai ei chael iddo’i hun. Ni fynnai hi ef, a ffodd i lawr y bryn tua’r eglwys. Marchogodd ar ei hôl, ei dal wrth ddrws yr eglwys, ac yn ei gynddaredd torrodd ei phen â’i gleddyf.",
   "Lle syrthiodd ei phen, tarddodd ffynnon o ddŵr gloyw o’r ddaear, mor gryf nes iddi lifo i’r môr. Daeth Beuno allan o’r eglwys, codi ei phen, ei osod yn ôl ar ei hysgwyddau, a gweddïo. Agorodd Gwenffrewi ei llygaid a chodi, yn fyw, a dim ond llinell wen fain o amgylch ei gwddf i ddangos lle’r aethai’r cleddyf. Suddodd Caradog, meddir, i’r ddaear ac ni welwyd ef byth wedyn.",
   "Bu Gwenffrewi fyw am flynyddoedd lawer wedyn, a daeth yn abades ar dŷ o wragedd sanctaidd yng Ngwytherin, yn y bryniau uwchlaw’r Conwy. Ond daliodd y ffynnon i lifo lle syrthiodd, ac roedd y cerrig ar ei gwaelod wedi’u staenio’n goch, a’r mwsogl o’i hamgylch yn arogli’n felys. Deuai pobl o bob rhan o Gymru i ymdrochi ynddi, a dywedai llawer iddynt gael eu hiacháu."],
  hEn="Gwenffrewi lived in the seventh century, and her well at Holywell, Treffynnon, the ‘town of the well’, has been a place of pilgrimage ever since: it is often said to be the oldest pilgrimage site in Britain still in use, and it is one of the Seven Wonders of Wales. The beautiful vaulted chapel over the well was built around 1500, by tradition at the cost of Lady Margaret Beaufort, mother of Harri Tudur. Even when pilgrimages were forbidden after the Reformation, people kept coming. Her feast day is 3 November, and pilgrims still bathe in the well today.",
  hCy="Roedd Gwenffrewi’n byw yn y seithfed ganrif, a bu ei ffynnon yn Nhreffynnon yn gyrchfan pererinion byth ers hynny: dywedir yn aml mai hi yw’r safle pererindod hynaf ym Mhrydain sy’n dal i gael ei ddefnyddio, ac mae’n un o Saith Rhyfeddod Cymru. Codwyd y capel hardd â’i do bwaog dros y ffynnon tua 1500, yn ôl traddodiad ar draul yr Arglwyddes Margaret Beaufort, mam Harri Tudur. Hyd yn oed pan waharddwyd pererindota wedi’r Diwygiad Protestannaidd, daliodd pobl i ddod. Ei gŵyl yw 3 Tachwedd, ac mae pererinion yn dal i ymdrochi yn y ffynnon heddiw."),
]

